  "answer_text": "# \ud83e\udd5b Calcium-Containing Foods\n\nCalcium is the most abundant mineral in your body and a major component of your bones and teeth. Since your body can't make calcium on its own, it's essential to consume calcium-rich foods to meet your daily calcium needs.\n\nCalcium is best known for building bones and keeping them healthy, but it also plays many other vital roles, like keeping your heart beating and your muscles and nerves functioning, as well as helping maintain healthy blood pressure.\n\n---\n\n## \ud83d\udc04 Dairy Products\nWhen you think of high-calcium foods, dairy products like **milk, yogurt, and cheese** probably come to mind first. Indeed, dairy foods are some of nature's most concentrated sources of calcium, and many come packaged with other bone-building nutrients like protein, phosphorus, and (when fortified) vitamin D.\n\n- \ud83e\udd5b **Milk** (whole, skim, or low-fat)\n- \ud83e\uddc0 **Cheese** (Swiss, cheddar, mozzarella) \u2014 a 3 cm cube of hard cheese has as much calcium as a cup of milk.\n- \ud83c\udf66 **Yogurt** (plain or low-fat)\n\n---\n\n## \ud83d\udc1f Fish & Seafood\n**Calcium-fortified orange juice** and **canned salmon or sardines** are excellent non-dairy sources. Pink canned salmon (3 oz) provides about **241 mg** of calcium.\n\n- \ud83d\udc1f Canned salmon (with bones)\n- \ud83d\udc20 Sardines (with bones)\n\n---\n\n## \ud83e\udd66 Vegetables & Greens\nMany plant foods are also calcium-rich, including **greens, beans, tofu, nuts, and seeds**.\n\n- \ud83e\udd6c **Kale, collard greens, bok choy** \u2014 these greens contain lower amounts of oxalic acid, so the calcium they contain is likely better absorbed.\n- \ud83e\udd66 **Broccoli**\n- \ud83c\udf3f **Turnip greens** \u2014 1 cup of cooked turnip greens provides about **209 mg** of calcium.\n\n---\n\n## \ud83c\udf31 Plant-Based & Legumes\nThose who avoid dairy products due to allergies may select non-dairy foods that contain calcium, such as **beans, tofu** (if processed with calcium sulfate), **broccoli, kale, and canned fish with bones**.\n\n- \ud83e\uded8 **Black beans** \u2014 ~239 mg per cup (raw)\n- \ud83e\udd5c **Almonds**\n- \ud83e\uded8 **Cowpeas/Black-eyed peas** \u2014 ~211 mg per cup (cooked)\n\n---\n\n## \ud83e\udd64 Fortified Foods & Beverages\n**Calcium-fortified soy, almond, and rice beverages** (check the nutrition labels) are also good sources of calcium.\n\n- Fortified plant-based milks (soy, almond, oat, rice)\n- Fortified orange juice\n- Fortified cereals and breads\n\n---\n\n## \u26a0\ufe0f Important Tips\n\n- Some healthy foods also contain compounds like insoluble fiber and oxalic acid that **bind with calcium and hinder its absorption**. (e.g., spinach, chard)\n- Calcium-rich foods and calcium-fortified foods are the **preferred choices** for obtaining calcium because additional nutrients like vitamin D in milk can contribute to bone development and prevention of osteoporosis.\n- Check food labels to find out the percentage of calcium in processed foods \u2014 **every 10% of calcium listed on the label is equivalent to approximately 100 mg of calcium.**",
